" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532558 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186532353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532558 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "89d19a91-9b5e-5bde-be4d-876637ef2257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532568 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532568 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532568 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532568 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532568 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532568 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532568 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532568 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186532439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532568 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a25a1262-2d6c-5d10-9f4a-f4ca15577a84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532626 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532626 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532626 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532626 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\pi $ in spherical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532626 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "151bdd66-5085-5cc9-a9af-25457fe9b069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532626 #edb/ref 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\pi \n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $\\pi .$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/41/415596385e885cf6c8fb553d126c550a78879d3503267bae102a8e038a688eb3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532627 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532627 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532627 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532627 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/91/917233475c847130eba1875f80606d91b03b53517fdb9273a3b32502619ef522.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532627 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16bc20a0-cc25-520a-9947-7c91335695c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532627 #edb/ref 1074 "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\frac{8\\pi }{9}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $ϕ=\\frac{8\\pi }{9}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532630 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532630 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532630 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b1/b15c12a1cabbab36feab2d15e1ac3d73e8d8c26afc14a8b820e2ca6f57d7d48d.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2z,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532630 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b4d1e3e-36ae-5b22-9e31-1455caee6c30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532630 #edb/ref 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(0,0,1).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2z$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2z \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/66/66812de081c1bbfe515162ab628806717b5602ec1a6cc61eaf22514f91e9c0ea.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532657 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532657 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532657 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532657 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7c923ff3fd6a74263ed05a0ace025e05b0e1b5c445c2bfb0801b8707ce9ac356.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532657 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "554c2eb9-1068-5995-9e2b-d384110d6d5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532657 #edb/ref 1074 "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532693 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532693 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532693 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d8/d829e8f51e5cd49bf20587f0cd70abd29a8dd1a336a6bad827541726c9cd4416.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4x,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532693 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7ea971b-d67e-5a60-86aa-35a475db9053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532693 #edb/ref 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/dfc7359ad44caa959459676a1e54bf1575e3c7e0f771e92a74817d9d1f9cb581.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532722 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532722 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532722 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532722 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho =1$ in spherical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532722 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f975b4e0-ae07-50ae-a2a8-6fe6de3927cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532722 #edb/ref 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =1\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $1.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](images/6b/6bca743f355735dae56d679cc601852f5a52bd5cc99e89a8d4f20a92111cf621.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
